## Radical innovation (advisory only; I did not change the plan)

7. **Add a bounded, exhaustive model check of the fold rule next to B18.** B18 samples two seeds. The creative decision admits that "no row strands forever" is "argued and simulated, not proven". A pure in-memory model can check every history up to a small size instead.
   - New test-only file `tests/test_fold_model.py`, with no I/O:
     - a row is a nested tuple tree of note numbers;
     - its sort key is its smallest leaf, and its grain is its leaf count.
   - `fold` calls the driver's own `m._fold_pair` on the grains, puts the merged row at the older row's position, and re-sorts.
   - `heal` copies `heal_view`'s rules on leaf sets:
     - take the first overlapping pair;
     - if one is a subset of the other, drop it;
     - otherwise split the smaller row into its two children.
   - Enumerate every history of 2 writers over N ≤ 8 notes (65,536 histories):
     - each note goes to writer A or B;
     - after each note, the writer either merges into `main` or does not;
     - use budget 2 or 3, so that folds happen even with few notes.
   - At every step, assert:
     - no stall while two power-of-two rows share a grain;
     - every grain is a power of two;
     - no leaf is lost.

   This turns the main correctness claim into a check of every case up to N. It uses the real picker, so the model cannot drift on the rule under test. The main risk is drift in the copied zipper; cross-check a few histories against the real driver. Cost: about 100 lines of test code and a few seconds of run time. B18 stays as the ratchet on the real driver.
